        "id": 1000018
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-12-20T19:17:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I2C SDA can\u0027t really be \"stuck high\".  Since SDA is Open-Drain, if the bus is high, either side can drive the bus low and both sides would then see low, so the bus isn\u0027t \"stuck\".  So, if the i2c slave is confused and the bus is high, the slave can always drive the bus low to signal a problem (cause an arb error, or NAK, or stop condition).  Thus, the bus can\u0027t be \"stuck high\".\n\nI2C can get \"stuck low\" when one side is actively driving low.  In this case the other side cannot modify SDA to signal that it is having a problem.  Hence the bus is stuck.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 45,
        "startChar": 33,
        "endLine": 45,
        "endChar": 50
      },
      "revId": "7efd46d698a48f8b60ac597514b6e6d9c5960999",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
